For each input/parameter, use the exact getter names listed in Integration. The parameter-free hot-start overloads are valid only for models without parameters. The configuration queries take no machine argument.{% if model.has_regions %} A model with regions generates no `current_state_id`, `current_state_path` or `current_state_name`; use `get_configuration`.{% endif %}

### `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}ExecutionContext`

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `state_id` | State the callsite is reported for; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_STATE_*` macros. An action inside a region reports its own state; an aspect action reports the deepest state common to everything it wraps, and a region's aspect reports its owner while that region has no active state |
| `vars` | Read-only snapshot pointer to persistent machine variables |
| `action_id` | Resolved abstract target action id; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_ACTION_*` macros |
| `action_stage_id` | Lifecycle stage id reported for this callsite; compare with `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_STAGE_*` |
| `active_leaf_state_id` | Same state as `state_id` for every callsite; a model with regions has no single active leaf, so read the position with `get_configuration` |
| `call_stage_id` | Lifecycle stage id of the concrete callsite before resolving `ref` chains |
| `abstract_target_id` | Resolved abstract action id after following `ref` chains |
| `named_ref_id` | Named `ref` action id when the callsite itself is a named `ref`, otherwise `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_INVALID_ACTION_ID`; a chain starting from an anonymous `ref` stays invalid however many named actions it passes through |

This structure is intended for observation only. Abstract hooks should not mutate persistent machine variables through `machine` or any other runtime-owned storage. All generation-time enumerable metadata is numeric on purpose: use the generated macros instead of string comparisons in hot paths.

### `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}EventContext`

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `event_id` | Queried event id; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_EVENT_*` macros |
{% if not model.has_regions %}| `current_state_id` | Current leaf-state id when the check runs; compare with generated `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_STATE_*` macros |
{% endif %}| `vars` | Read-only snapshot pointer to persistent machine variables |

This structure is the read-only observation surface for event checks. Event- check callbacks should be treated as probes, not as mutation points for persistent machine state. Event and state metadata are numeric{% if model.has_regions %} (a model with regions has no single current state, so the context has no `current_state_id` field){% endif %} so polling code can stay allocation-free and avoid string comparisons.

<a id="build"></a>

## Build

### Deployment Profiles

The generated runtime supports three integration profiles:

1. **Default hosted C99**: keep `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}_create()` and `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}_destroy()` available. This profile may use the C standard library and is suitable for hosted systems such as desktop tools, industrial PCs, embedded Linux, or hosted RTOS builds.
2. **Caller-owned object**: allocate `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}` yourself, for example on the stack or in static storage, and call `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}_init(&machine)` or `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}_hot_start(...)`. This is the preferred path when object lifetime is controlled by a driver, scheduler, or device owner.
3. **No-heap profile**: define `PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP` for both `machine.c` and every source file that includes `machine.h`. In this profile the heap API declarations and implementations are omitted, and `machine.c` does not include `<stdlib.h>` for `calloc` / `free`.

`PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP` is tested by symbol presence, using `#if defined(PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP)` / `#ifdef` semantics. The documented build form is `-DPYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP`; an external build that spells it as `-DPYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP=1` must still select the same no-heap profile.

### Object Lifetime Examples

```c
{{ machine_class_name(model) }} stack_machine;
static {{ machine_class_name(model) }} static_machine;

if (!{{ machine_class_name(model) }}_init(&stack_machine) ||
    !{{ machine_class_name(model) }}_init(&static_machine)) {
    fprintf(stderr, "initialization failed\n");
    return 1;
}
```

The default hosted profile also allows heap-managed objects:

```c
{{ machine_class_name(model) }} *machine = {{ machine_class_name(model) }}_create();
if (machine == NULL) {
    return 1;
}
{{ machine_class_name(model) }}_destroy(machine);
```

<a id="capacity"></a>

### Capacity macros

The machine reserves its private storage statically, so no heap is needed. Two public macros in `machine.h` size it, and their defaults suit this model:

| Macro | Bounds | Raise it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `{{ a }}_ARENA_CAPACITY` | How many configurations (and pending work items) one decision of a cycle may examine | A cycle fails with `Speculative search exceeded the capacity safety limit` and the message names this macro |
| `{{ a }}_TRAIL_CAPACITY` | How many checks may be nested | The same failure names this macro |

`{{ a }}_STORAGE_BYTES` is derived from both. Because the size of a machine depends on them, define a changed value identically for `machine.c` and every source file that includes `machine.h`, for example with a compiler flag; a mismatch gives the two sides different machine sizes. A model that fits its defaults never needs this, and a value below what the model needs makes a cycle fail with the message above instead of misbehaving.

```bash
cc -std=c99 -D{{ a }}_ARENA_CAPACITY=480 -D{{ a }}_TRAIL_CAPACITY=96 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 machine.c app.c -lm -o app
```

### Single-command C Builds

The examples below are validated with gcc / clang style command lines. Compile the generated runtime and your application together:

```bash
cc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 machine.c app.c -lm -o app
```

Equivalent explicit gcc / clang forms are:

```bash
gcc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 machine.c app.c -lm -o app
clang -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 machine.c app.c -lm -o app
```

For a no-heap build, define the same profile symbol for the generated runtime and every consumer that includes `machine.h`:

```bash
cc -std=c99 -DPYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -O2 machine.c app.c -lm -o app
```

A separate object build is also valid:

```bash
cc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -c machine.c -o machine.o
cc -std=c99 -Wall -Wextra -c app.c -o app.o
cc machine.o app.o -lm -o app
```

On Windows, use the equivalent library or DLL flags for your compiler toolchain and do not force-link the POSIX math library `m`.

### CMake Integration

A minimal static-library integration can look like this:

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.5)
project(generated_machine_app C)

add_library(machine STATIC machine.c)
target_include_directories(machine PUBLIC ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR})
set_target_properties(
    machine
    PROPERTIES
    C_STANDARD 99
    C_STANDARD_REQUIRED YES
    C_EXTENSIONS NO
)

if(NOT WIN32)
    target_link_libraries(machine PUBLIC m)
endif()

add_executable(app app.c)
target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE machine)
```

For a no-heap library that is consumed by other targets, propagate the macro as part of the public interface:

```cmake
target_compile_definitions(machine PUBLIC PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP)
```

If `machine.c` is compiled into an interface-style wrapper, use `INTERFACE PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP` on the wrapper target. A `PRIVATE` definition is only safe when `machine.c` and all consumer source files that include `machine.h` are compiled in the same final target and therefore see the same macro.

### Integration limits

Serialize access to each instance: it is non-reentrant. Do not call cycle recursively from callbacks. Keep volatile device registers, DMA buffers and I/O inside application adapters and expose numeric readings through input providers. `PYFCSTM_GENERATED_NO_HEAP` removes heap helpers; it is not a strict freestanding guarantee. Standard math, string and diagnostic facilities may still be required.

Before C/C++ deployment, run `pyfcstm inspect` and review numeric diagnostics for the default C/C++ deployment-profile; Python numeric behavior differs. Generated `round()` uses `nearbyint()` and assumes `FE_TONEAREST`. Integer overflow, target ABI and cross-device serialization need application-level review. Compiler and sanitizer evidence does not establish MISRA, AUTOSAR, DO-178C, IEC 61508 or ISO 26262 certification.

### Linkage and Visibility

- `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_API` is the generated symbol-visibility macro used by all public functions.
- Define `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_BUILD_SHARED` when building the generated runtime itself as a Windows DLL.
- Define `{{ machine_macro_name(model) }}_USE_SHARED` when consuming that DLL from external code on Windows.
- `machine.h` wraps only the public API declarations in `extern "C"` when included from C++.
- Public layout structs, hook typedefs, and event-check typedefs stay usable from both C and C++.
- In generated integer fields, `{{ machine_class_name(model) }}Int` is the public signed 64-bit type alias chosen to stay valid in both C99 and C++98+.

